  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain associated with the remnants of Hurricane Isaias \narrives late Saturday morning and with widespread rainfall\nexpected by Saturday evening.\n\nSurface high pressure will continue to retreat to the northeast\nover New England through Saturday, which will create a robust\nwedging setup across the region. This will lead to much of the\nPiedmont under much cooler temperatures, with rainy conditions\nstarting Saturday afternoon and continuing through Sunday\nafternoon for much of the area. Rainfall should start across\nportions of NW North Carolina by the mid morning hours Saturday,\nand continue to spread north and eastward to encompass the \nremainder of the area by the afternoon and early evening hours \non Saturday. In general, much of the forecast area is likely to\nsee 1-2 inches of rainfall, with higher totals expected along\nand east of the Blue Ridge due to upslope flow, and lower totals\nwest of the Blue Ridge given a rain shadow effect. \n\nFavored regions for higher totals look to be along portions of \nthe Blue Ridge as a 50-60kt southeasterly 850mb LLJ pushes into\nthe area as the remnants of Isaias move into the Tennessee \nvalley. Upslope flow from this LLJ will likely lead to increased\nrainfall totals into the 2 to 4 inches range, with perhaps some\nlocalized higher amounts in the 5 to 6 inch range particularly \nin northwest North Carolina. This general area has the greatest \nconcern for flooding given the upslope enhancement, along with \nincreasing deep tropical moisture (PWAT values nearing 2\"), \nwhich could result in periods of higher intensity rain rates. \nTimeframe for the highest rainfall rates based on REFS guidance \nwill be late Saturday night into early Sunday morning. A Flood \nWatch has now been issued for this region extending from Floyd\nand Patrick county in SW Virginia towards Wilkes and Watauga\ncounty in NW North Carolina. \n\nThe aforementioned 850mb LLJ is expected to lead to a downslope\nwind event for areas in SE West Virginia and SW Virginia. Hi-res\nCAM guidance from the RRFS have probabilities of 50%-80% for\nwinds gusting to greater than 50 mph across this western\ncorridor of the CWA. This has prompted the issuance of a Wind\nAdvisory for these areas, with the possibility of seeing\nisolated gusts to near 60 mph along ridge lines above 3500 ft\nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Wind gusts should diminish\nthrough Sunday morning and afternoon as the LLJ lifts north and\neast of the region. \n\nTrends for a Sunday afternoon severe weather threat, with the\npossibility of an isolated tornado for most of the NC and VA \nPiedmont is increasing with models now hinting at high pressure \nretreating enough to allow for a brief period of destabilization\nalong a triple point in Central Virginia. Strong advection of\nmoisture due to the LLJ will raise dewpoints enough across the\narea to allow for minor destabilization to occur across this \nmentioned region. Along with the minor destabilization, the LLJ \nmay support rotating low-topped supercells given 0-1km SRH \nexceeding 200m2s2 and Bulk Shear values of 40-50kts.\n\nBy late Monday, all remnants of Isaias look to be well east of\nthe region, with dry conditions returning areawide once again. A\npossible dry frontal passage by the Tuesday night timeframe\nlooks to drop temperatures slightly through the middle of the\nwork week. Overall, the region will likely not see any rain\nchances through most of next week after the remnants from \nIsaias move through over the weekend.",
